          "source": "def-motivic-stack-function-groups-for-quiver-representations",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Fix a field $k$. An Artin $k$-stack is as in\n[[def-algebraic-stack-and-inertia]]. In this item, a finite-type stack means an\nArtin $k$-stack with a finite-type smooth atlas and finite-type diagonal. It\nhas affine stabilizers when the automorphism group scheme of every geometric\npoint is affine.\n\nFor a finite-type Artin stack $X$ with affine stabilizers, define\n$\\mathscr K(X)$ to be the abelian group generated by isomorphism classes\n$[Y\\to X]$ of finite-type Artin stacks $Y$ with affine stabilizers and a\n1-morphism to $X$. Impose the following relations.\n\n1. If $Z\\hookrightarrow Y$ is a closed substack, then\n   $[Y\\to X]=[Z\\to X]+[Y\\setminus Z\\to X]$.\n2. Let $S\\to X$ be a finite-type stack with affine stabilizers and let\n   $d:V_0\\to V_1$ be a constant-rank morphism of vector bundles on $S$.\n   The additive vector bundle $V_0$ acts on $V_1$ by translation through\n   $d$. Write $[V_1/V_0\\to S\\to X]$ for the resulting quotient groupoid\n   stack over $X$. If $r_i$ is the rank of $V_i$, impose\n   $[V_1/V_0\\to X]=\\mathbb L^{r_1-r_0}[S\\to X]$.\n\nHere $\\mathbb L$ is the class of $\\mathbb A^1_k$ in\n$R:=\\mathscr K(\\operatorname{Spec}k)$, and we localize $R$ and the modules\n$\\mathscr K(X)$ by making $\\mathbb L$ invertible. The product in $R$ is\ninduced by product over $k$. The group $\\mathscr K(X)$ is an $R$-module.\nThis explicit vector-bundle-stack relation records the class of a family of\nlinear objects modulo its linear changes of splitting.\n\nFor a morphism $f:X\\to X'$, pullback is\n$f^*[Y\\to X']=[Y\\times_{X'}X\\to X]$ and pushforward is\n$f_![Z\\to X]=[Z\\to X\\to X']$. Exterior product is\n$[Y\\to X]\\boxtimes[Z\\to X']=[Y\\times_k Z\\to X\\times_kX']$.\nThese operations are well-defined because fiber product and composition\npreserve isomorphisms, closed-substack decompositions, and the\nvector-bundle-stack relation. If the rank of a vector-bundle map varies, its\nbase is partitioned into the finitely many locally closed rank strata and\nthe scissor relation is applied on those strata.\n\nThe dependency on [[def-axiom-of-choice]] is inherited from the cited\nalgebraic-stack convention in [[def-algebraic-stack-and-inertia]], specifically\nits represented-sheaf descent foundation. No choice is used in the\nfinite-dimensional quiver matrix calculations below.",
