          "source": "def-motivic-hall-algebra-of-a-finite-quiver",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $Q=(Q_0,Q_1,s,t)$ be a finite quiver over a field $k$. A representation\nof $Q$ assigns a finite-dimensional $k$-vector space $V_i$ to each vertex\n$i\\in Q_0$ and a linear map $V_{s(a)}\\to V_{t(a)}$ to each arrow\n$a\\in Q_1$. Its dimension vector is\n$\\mathbf d=(\\dim V_i)_{i\\in Q_0}\\in\\mathbb N^{Q_0}$.\n\nFor $\\mathbf d\\in\\mathbb N^{Q_0}$ put\n$$\\operatorname{Rep}_{\\mathbf d}(Q) =\\prod_{a\\in Q_1}\\operatorname{Hom}_k (k^{d_{s(a)}},k^{d_{t(a)}}),\\qquad G_{\\mathbf d}=\\prod_{i\\in Q_0}\\operatorname{GL}_{d_i}(k),$$and let $\\mathfrak M_{\\mathbf d}(Q)=[\\operatorname{Rep}_{\\mathbf d}(Q)/ G_{\\mathbf d}]$ be the quotient Artin stack. It is finite type and has affine stabilizers: the representation variety and the acting group are finite type and affine, and each stabilizer is a closed subgroup of $G_{\\mathbf d}$. For dimension vectors $\\mathbf d,\\mathbf e$, let $\\mathfrak E_{\\mathbf d,\\mathbf e}(Q)$ be the stack of short exact sequences of representations$$0\\longrightarrow B_{\\mathbf e}\\longrightarrow E_{\\mathbf d+\\mathbf e} \\longrightarrow A_{\\mathbf d}\\longrightarrow0.$$The endpoints are part of the data, and isomorphisms are isomorphisms of the whole exact sequence. Equivalently, after choosing the standard $\\mathbf e$-dimensional subspaces at each vertex, this is the quotient by the product parabolic preserving those subspaces of the affine space of block upper-triangular arrow maps. Thus $\\mathfrak E_{\\mathbf d,\\mathbf e}(Q)$ is a finite-type Artin stack with affine stabilizers. More explicitly, on a local splitting $E_i=B_i\\oplus A_i$, the arrow $a:i\\to j$ has block matrix$$\\begin{pmatrix} B_a & \\xi_a\\\\ 0 & A_a \\end{pmatrix}, \\qquad \\xi_a:A_i\\to B_j.$$Changing the splitting by $h_i:A_i\\to B_i$ changes $\\xi_a$ to $\\xi_a+B_a h_i-h_j A_a$. This is the translation action of the vector bundle $\\bigoplus_i\\operatorname{Hom}(A_i,B_i)$ on $\\bigoplus_{a:i\\to j}\\operatorname{Hom}(A_i,B_j)$ used in the extension stack calculation. Let $q:\\mathfrak E_{\\mathbf d,\\mathbf e}\\to\\mathfrak M_{\\mathbf d}$ send a sequence to its quotient, $s:\\mathfrak E_{\\mathbf d,\\mathbf e}\\to \\mathfrak M_{\\mathbf e}$ send it to its subrepresentation, and $m:\\mathfrak E_{\\mathbf d,\\mathbf e}\\to\\mathfrak M_{\\mathbf d+\\mathbf e}$ send it to its middle representation. The Hall correspondence is\n\n~~~tikzcd\n& \\mathfrak E_{\\mathbf d,\\mathbf e}(Q) \\ar[dl,\"(q,s)\"'] \\ar[dr,\"m\"] & \\\\ \\mathfrak M_{\\mathbf d}(Q)\\times\\mathfrak M_{\\mathbf e}(Q) && \\mathfrak M_{\\mathbf d+\\mathbf e}(Q)\n~~~\n\nIts arrows record the ordered quotient, subrepresentation, and middle object of the displayed short exact sequence; the diagram fixes the convolution convention. Define$$H_Q:=\\bigoplus_{\\mathbf d\\in\\mathbb N^{Q_0}} \\mathscr K(\\mathfrak M_{\\mathbf d}(Q)).$$For $\\alpha\\in\\mathscr K(\\mathfrak M_{\\mathbf d})$ and $\\beta\\in\\mathscr K(\\mathfrak M_{\\mathbf e})$, define$$\\alpha*\\beta :=m_!\\big((q,s)^*(\\alpha\\boxtimes\\beta)\\big) \\in\\mathscr K(\\mathfrak M_{\\mathbf d+\\mathbf e}).$$\nThus the first factor is the quotient and the second factor is the\nsubrepresentation, so the sequence is\n$0\\to B\\to E\\to A\\to0$. Each homogeneous product is defined by one\nfinite-type stack correspondence; no completion or finiteness of a sum over\nisomorphism classes is required. This is the stack-function product without\na potential or a vanishing-cycle weight.",
